[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-35751"] :question/problem "Consider the set of matrices ${A,B},$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -12\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf the set is linearly dependent, find the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}$ from a non-zero solution of ${x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B=O,$ where $O$ is the $2\\times 2$ zero-matrix." :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A$ and $B$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 10\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -12\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1} & 5{x}_{1} \\\\ -{x}_{1} & 10{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{2} & 6{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & -12{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & 5{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & 10{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}/2\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-10){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. Therefore, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 16{x}_{2}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich has only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\nConsequently, ${A,B}$ is linearly independent." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94779/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "9535f521-fb4a-5769-9076-2effa2acbb5d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94779/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "9e75fed4-d478-5a86-817d-d77e9e6e93c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94779/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "52183ace-92c7-56bc-8776-c91b11c400f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94779/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "2eaa76a7-fc78-5e34-9000-d521ccc0a082" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94779/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "d9c1ab53-475e-5d20-89eb-d0b35c95dbe4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94779/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a54f71a-479b-5d56-b285-ef5dc5c6a0eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94779/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-94779/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-94779/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-94779/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-94779/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-94779/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94779" :question/id #uuid "93fea67e-a3d6-502c-9ac5-3b5196ad6f92" :question/math-academy-id "q-94779" :question/problem "Consider a vector space $V$ and vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\in V.$ Given that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly independent\n- ${{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given options in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{1}+{v}_{3})+{x}_{3}{v}_{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, in turn, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){v}_{1}+({x}_{1}+{x}_{3}){v}_{2}+{x}_{2}{v}_{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set, the final equation can be true only if\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe unique solution of the system is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ which means that \n\n$$\n{{v}_{1}+{v}_{2},{v}_{1}+{v}_{3},{v}_{2}}\n$$\n\n is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n1\\cdot ({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+1\\cdot ({v}_{2}-{v}_{1})=0.\n$$\n\nAs a result, ${x}_{1}({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})+{x}_{2}({v}_{2}-{v}_{1})=0$ has a non-zero solution. This means that ${{v}_{1}-{v}_{2},{v}_{2}-{v}_{1}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, ${x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0$ must have only the zero solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$  If this equation has a non-zero solution, $a\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\na\\cdot {v}_{1}+b\\cdot {v}_{2}+0\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwhich would mean that ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94779/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "486cc512-416b-5eac-8964-1a87d533de72"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94779"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
